A||||15||AkursedX||Ok, What else should I mod/replace while it's apart?||||5||
Z||000000||AkursedX||07-15-2001||08:46 PM||||Well, here's what my engine looks like right now:<BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7150013.JPG"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7150014.JPG"> <BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/P7150015.JPG"> <P>Well in the post entitled "I hate my life, failed Michigan trip"  you will see the reason why I had to tear my car apart like this.  I think I found the problem though.  One of the lifters looks alot different than the rest of them. So I think that is the culprit.<P>So, at the minimum, I will be replacing the lifters, pushrods and rocker arms.  I am looking for high performance stuff to replace these.  Any idea on lifters?  I'm pretty sure I found good pushrods.<P>And does anyone have any suggestions on rocker arms?  1.52, 1.6, roller??  <P>Plus, what else should I modify/replace while everything is off?  I am looking to spend about 500 bucks in parts.  And I figure the lifters, gaskets, rockers and push rods will be about 300-350 bucks.  So that gives me some room to spend on extras.<P>Any input is greatly appreciated  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">  <P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||5
Z||000001||Mark||07-15-2001||08:54 PM||||Well, you just gave me a flashback to what my engine looked like last fall.  I tore it apart to change the valve seals (about $16 for the actual valve seals, about another $100 or so for all the other gaskets, seals, paint, etc.<P>So, if you are getting a puff of smoke on cold startup (and if that annoys you), now is a good time to install a new valve seal kit. <p>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 07-15-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000002||AkursedX||07-15-2001||09:00 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Mark:<BR><B>Well, you just gave me a flashback to what my engine looked like last fall.  I tore it apart to change the valve seals (about $16 for the actual valve seals, about another $100 or so for all the other gaskets, seals, paint, etc.<P>So, if you are getting a puff of smoke on cold startup (and if that annoys you), now is a good time to install a new valve seal kit. <P>[This message has been edited by Mark (edited 07-15-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>The valve seals are good thank god.  The heads were previousley rebuilt before I bought it, so nothing in the heads are really in bad shape.  I thought about pulling them, but everything is REALLY clean, so I'm gonna save some work and keep them on<P>||||reg||1
Z||000003||Carrolles||07-16-2001||08:25 AM||||You might want to gasket match port the intake and exhaust manifolds while you have them off.  That's free hp waiting to be had.<P>It's pretty easy to do especially ifyou have an air die grinder and some carbide bits.||||reg||1
Z||000004||skitime||07-16-2001||08:59 AM||||You could put on 1.6 roller rockers for a performance boost.<P>------------------<BR><UL TYPE=SQUARE><BR><LI>Red 88 GT T-TOP<BR><LI>Red 86 GT<BR></UL> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/signature-1.JPG"> ||||reg||1
Z||000005||Dumba$$||07-16-2001||11:02 AM||||i know that this is off topic  from your question but i really like your deck lid <BR>could you tell me about it<BR>what you used for a vent and glass?<P>fierogod and myself   ok mostly him<BR>will be starting on my 4.9 project next week <BR>in my gold 86se and i think that would look really good <P><P>------------------<BR>Rich<BR>Sioux Falls SD<BR>Red 84 SE<BR>Gold 86 SE||||reg||1
Z||000006||Dave Gunsul||07-16-2001||03:43 PM||||1.6 rockers would help you out but if you're going to do this then change the valve springs for a high perf. type or you will bind. Crane and Comp. Cams both make good<BR>ones that will work.<BR>Comp. Cams sells their roller tipped rockers in a kit that includes matching pushrods.<BR>You can get these special order from Summit<BR>but it will take a while.<P>Also: if you're going to do the 1.6's you'll have to decide whether to use full rollers or<BR>roller tipped rockers.<BR>The roller tipped rockers cost far less than the full rollers do.<P>As someone mentioned above: this is a great time to do some porting work on your lower intake. The lower intake is the most restrictive part of the intake. Also a good time to get ported exh. manifolds.<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||1
Z||000007||Black88GT||07-16-2001||03:55 PM||||I would go with 1.52 roller rockers over 1.6.  1.6 will cause premature valve guide wear (Mike L. learned this and passed along to me).  Also go full roller over just roller tip.  May be more expensive but worth it.  Ported throttle body/plenum would help too, morse86@aol.com is Darrell Morse, he does them.  He is having a special, 100 bucks with free return shipping.  He bores his out more then the Fiero store also and sand blasts them, included in the price.  <P>Oh and replace that filter with a K&N!! hehe||||reg||1
Z||000008||Raydar||07-16-2001||07:21 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by AkursedX:<BR><B>...I think I found the problem though.  One of the lifters looks alot different than the rest of them. So I think that is the culprit.<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Is the face of the lifter worn? Or is the lifter just collapsed? If the face is worn, you need to look at the cam carefully. It might have damaged a lobe. (They're not roller lifters, are they?)<BR>I would also be curious as to what caused it to fail in the first place. <BR>Maybe someone else will offer an opinion, as I've never been into one of our motors, just stuff that I've seen/read.<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-16-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000009||AkursedX||07-16-2001||10:28 PM||||Well, I ordered all the parts I needed.  For rockers, I just ordered the 1.52 roller tip rockers, I couldn't afford better ones.  I got new lifters and pushrods.  I also bought a low temp fan switch from Rodney Dickman, a new pickup coil since the distributor is out, a K and N air filter, and some other small stuff.<P>I also ported my lower intake.  I hope that'll give me a couple extra horses.  I might do the exhaust manifolds too.  I will see if I can get them off tomorrow.  <P>All in all, this job will cost me about 400 bucks.  Not too bad.  I just hope I will have a little more power, and a good running car afterwards.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><BR><P>------------------<BR><BR> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/sigpic2_2.jpg"> ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Dave Gunsul||07-16-2001||11:40 PM||||Not bad on the price!<BR>Good luck and let us know how it turns out!<P>------------------<BR>Dave Gunsul<BR>N.I.F.E. member<BR>86 GT modified<BR>85 SE daily driver||||reg||14
Z||000011||AkursedX||07-17-2001||12:01 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>  <BR>I would also be curious as to what caused it to fail in the first place. <P><BR></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Failure was caused by running at 75+mph for about 2 hours straight on a 80-degrees day running 5w-30 Mobil-1 synthetic oil.  The oil got too thin, and when I stopped for about 15 minutes at idle while I was waiting to go through customs, The oil was so thin that the lifter wasn't getting oiled properly.  <P>20w50 oil, and some marvel mystery oil cleared up most of the problem, but it didn't go away completely.  <P>||||reg||1
Z||000012||Raydar||07-17-2001||12:16 AM||||I don't think that *should* have caused a lifter to fail. Not to say that it didn't.<BR>Possibly some clearances on the main bearings have opened up a bit from where they should be. Could possibly starve the cam and lifters. Just out of curiosity, was the bad lifter on the "water pump" end of the engine? <BR>Might pay you to install a high volume oil pump. <P>Anyway, this is all just speculation. <BR>Good luck with whatever you do.<BR>Sweet lookin' ride!<P>------------------<BR>Raydar - aka Steve<BR><FONT SIZE="1"><BR>Black 88 Formula.<BR>Red 88 Duke coupe. "The Project" <BR>88 Formula parts car. "The Donor"<BR></FONT><p>[This message has been edited by Raydar (edited 07-17-2001).]||||reg||1
Z||000013||AkursedX||07-17-2001||12:31 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Raydar:<BR><B>Just out of curiosity, was the bad lifter on the "water pump" end of the engine? <BR>Might pay you to install a high volume oil pump. <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Yeah, it was on the waterpump end of the engine.  And I will look into a high volume pump when I rebuild the engine in the winter.<P>This stuff right now is basically temporary until I have the time and money for my total performance rebuild.  <P>And yeah, the main bearings and everything else on the bottom end is wearing I'm sure.  The engine has 95k on it.  <P>And to be honest, I didn't think that those conditions would cause a lifter failure either, but it did.  <BR>||||reg||1
Z||000014||grinthock||07-17-2001||07:30 AM||||I didn't WITNESS it, but last year on the way BACK from the MI show another guy has the same problem, fixed by putting in 20W50 oil.......<P>A Mech who was with us actually told me he reccomends a high vol oil pump, he put them in a couple guys cars here, nice pressure... How hard is it to change ?||||reg||1
Z||000015||84Bill||07-17-2001||07:47 AM||||Put a four cylinder in it!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>I just couldnt resist.||||reg||14
